## State Summary

Self-provisioned the read-only observability service (`observability-readonly`, serviceId `cmv5t9tgo009fyxghmn7axqse`, endpoint `http://127.0.0.1:3114/mcp`) from the descriptor at the URL named in `serviceDescriptors["observability-config"]`, fetched via the Browser Automation Service and registered with its 10 declared read-only tools. Issued 5 narrow, scoped read-only calls: `get_ingress_config`, `get_otel_config`, `stack_health`, `get_scrape_targets`, `get_rules`. **All reads succeeded — no denials encountered** (nothing on this allowlist was out-of-policy for this harvest). Registration is **left active**; teardown delete is the harness's job at SYNTHESIZE, not mine. Stack confirmed **live**: `prometheus: up, grafana: up, otel_collector: up`, 5/5 scrape targets healthy, 0 dropped targets — sufficient liveness evidence; `get_prometheus_config` was not additionally needed since the primary objective (ingress + OTLP receiver config) was already fully witnessed.

## Declared OTLP receiver endpoints (witnessed via `get_otel_config`, as-deployed file mount)

```yaml
# source: get_otel_config — as-deployed file (ro mount), verbatim
receivers:
  otlp:
    protocols:
      grpc: { endpoint: 172.31.114.10:4317 }
      http: { endpoint: 172.31.114.10:4318 }
```
Declared sender targets: **gRPC `172.31.114.10:4317`**, **HTTP `172.31.114.10:4318`**.

## Ingress proxy — as-deployed (witnessed via `get_ingress_config`, as-deployed file mount — rollback-quotable: yes, the file IS the artifact)

**`otlp-ingress.conf`** (verbatim):
```nginx
# OTLP/gRPC
server {
    listen 4317;
    http2 on;

    location / {
        allow all;

        grpc_pass grpc://172.31.114.10:4317;
    }
}

# OTLP/http
server {
    listen 4318;
    client_max_body_size 16m;

    location / {
        allow all;

        proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $remote_addr;
    }
}
```

**`operator-access.conf`** (verbatim):
```nginx
server {
    listen 14318;
    client_max_body_size 16m;

    location / {
        allow 172.20.0.1;
        deny all;

        proxy_pass http://172.31.114.10:4318;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header Host $host;
    }
}
```

## Classification — by forward target, per `senderEndpointRule` (regardless of block description)

| File / listen port | Forward target (proxy_pass / grpc_pass) | Matches declared OTLP receiver? | Classification | Existing access directives |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `otlp-ingress.conf` — `listen 4317` (gRPC) | `grpc://172.31.114.10:4317` | = declared gRPC receiver | **SENDER block** | `allow all;` only — no `deny` line (brownfield baseline, unrestricted) |
| `otlp-ingress.conf` — `listen 4318` (HTTP) | `http://172.31.114.10:4318` | = declared HTTP receiver | **SENDER block** | `allow all;` only — no `deny` line (brownfield baseline, unrestricted) |
| `operator-access.conf` — `listen 14318` | `http://172.31.114.10:4318` | = declared HTTP receiver | **SENDER block** (per forward-target rule — see finding below) | `allow 172.20.0.1; deny all;` — already present and restrictive |

**Non-sender forwarding blocks found: none.** All three server blocks on the ingress proxy forward to one of the two declared OTLP receiver endpoints; there is no forwarding block to report under "leave unchanged" handling for this harvest.

## ⚠️ Critical finding — `operator-access.conf` classifies as a sender block despite its own description

`operator-access.conf`'s own comment explicitly disclaims sender-allowlist status ("rig plumbing, NOT part of the sender allowlist… A change authorising OTLP senders has no reason to touch this file"). However, its `proxy_pass` target is `http://172.31.114.10:4318` — **byte-identical to the declared OTLP/HTTP receiver endpoint** and to the forward target of the `otlp-ingress.conf` HTTP block. Per the interface contract's `senderEndpointRule` ("classified by forward target… whatever the block's own description says") and `enforcerScope` ("covers every part of the proxy that reaches the collector's OTLP receivers — all-reaching-the-protected-thing"), this block **must be classified as a sender block**, not a non-sender path, regardless of its stated intent.

This creates a design tension for the downstream Architect to resolve (not something I resolve or round off here): `operator-access.conf` already carries working restrictive directives (`allow 172.20.0.1; deny all;`) serving a distinct purpose — admitting the rig host's gateway address for operator curl-based demo injection, on a **different listen port** (14318) than the two OTLP-facing ports (4317/4318). The contract's `existingGrantBehavior` ("replace: on every sender block, allowances become exactly the chained sender set, followed by `deny all`") and `collateral` ("confined-to-block: no other server block, location, port or forwarded path on the proxy changes") both bear on how this third block should be treated. I report the fact and the conflict; I do not decide it.

## UNTESTED premise — admitted-principal request path not independently confirmed

The contract's `admittedPrincipal.requestPath` states senders reach the proxy "direct — each admitted sender's request reaches the proxy carrying its own pod address (item 13)." This harvest (nginx config files + Prometheus/Grafana/collector reads) has **no visibility into the cluster network path** between the trading-namespace pods and the ingress proxy, and cannot confirm whether requests arrive with the pod's own source address unmodified (vs. behind a NAT/LB that would rewrite it). Per branch condition `observability-config.principal-unseen`, this premise should be carried forward as **owner-declared, not observed** — act-regardless, but the downstream deliverable must state it as such under UNTESTED premises.

## Supplementary liveness evidence (secondary objective)

`get_scrape_targets`: 5/5 targets `health: up`, 0 dropped (`grafana:3000`, `node-exporter:9100`, `otel-collector:8888`, `otel-collector:8889`, `localhost:9090`).
`get_rules`: 2 rule groups loaded (`node-exporter-host-metrics`, `platform-baseline`) — recording + alerting rules present and in `inactive` state (no current firing alerts), confirming the stack is functioning normally and unrelated to the ingress change under design.

## Gaps / notes
- No reads were denied by the allowlist — nothing to report as an expected denial.
- `get_prometheus_config` was not called; liveness was already fully evidenced by `stack_health` + `get_scrape_targets`, and Prometheus config is not part of this task's primary objective (ingress + OTLP receiver config).
- Registration `observability-readonly` (serviceId `cmv5t9tgo009fyxghmn7axqse`) intentionally left in place for downstream Phase 1/2 use if needed; teardown is the harness's SYNTHESIZE-time responsibility.

Confidence: 92 — All primary-objective reads (ingress config, OTLP receiver config, stack liveness) succeeded and are quoted verbatim per the witnessed-artifact taxonomy; the one explicit gap (admitted-principal request-path confirmation) is named as an UNTESTED premise rather than assumed, and the operator-access.conf classification finding is reported as fact per the contract's own forward-target rule rather than resolved.

